# tools/crypto_hmac_sha256_witness.rish — HMAC-SHA-256 authored in pure Rye: Season G's keyed-hash rung, the message-authentication
# and key-derivation stone the deployed world reaches for first (TLS 1.3 PRF, JWT HS256, TOTP, Signal/Bitcoin HKDF), proven against
# the RFC 4231 known-answer and byte-for-byte against an independent implementation (Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G · the crypto parity wave).
# Kin: crypto/sha256.rye (the hash beneath it, GREEN) · crypto/hmac_sha512.rye (its 512-bit sibling). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher,
# CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity discipline target, never a copied line — our own Rye, studied clean-room from the public standards and
# RFC 4231 (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/hmac_sha256.rye composes the GREEN crypto/sha256.rye through the RFC 2104 / FIPS 198-1 HMAC construction — the key brought to
# the 64-byte block (hashed first when longer, then zero-padded), the inner pass over (K0 XOR 0x36) ‖ message, the outer pass over
# (K0 XOR 0x5c) ‖ inner-digest — authoring no new cryptography. The primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity is proven at selftest against
# two references runnable now: the RFC 4231 Test Case 2 known-answer (key "Jefe", "what do ya want for nothing?") hex for hex, and Zig's
# own std.crypto HMAC-SHA-256 as an independent implementation, byte-for-byte across a short key, a block-length key, a key longer than
# the block (the hash-the-key path), the empty message, and a multi-block message. An over-long message refuses MessageTooLong and an
# over-long key refuses KeyTooLong before a byte is mixed. Purely local — TEST keys and public RFC vectors, no real identity key, no
# network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/crypto_hmac_sha256_witness.rish

say "crypto-hmac-sha256: Language EN — Season G's keyed-hash rung: HMAC-SHA-256 (RFC 2104 / FIPS 198-1), the standard 'did this message come from a holder of the key, unaltered?' primitive and the exact stone the deployed world reaches for first — TLS 1.3's PRF, JWT's HS256, TOTP one-time codes, the Signal / Bitcoin HKDF. It authors no new hashing — it composes the GREEN crypto/sha256.rye through the HMAC construction, so it is genuinely buildable this round and carries a published RFC 4231 known-answer that ties parity to a neutral public reference."
say "crypto-hmac-sha256: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: HMAC(K, m) = H((K0 XOR opad) ‖ H((K0 XOR ipad) ‖ m)), the key first brought to the 64-byte SHA-256 block, ipad the byte 0x36 repeated, opad the byte 0x5c repeated, so the same key and bytes always fold to the same 32-byte tag anyone can replay against the standard."
say "crypto-hmac-sha256: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std.crypto; it composes an already-GREEN hash, bounded message (max_message_bytes) and bounded key (max_key_bytes), contract asserts on every function, the two SHA-256 calls beneath provably within their own bound so their error is unreachable and asserted rather than swallowed. Parity holds against the RFC 4231 Test Case 2 known-answer AND Zig's independent std.crypto HMAC-SHA-256 byte-for-byte across a short key, a block-length key, a key longer than the block, the empty message, and a multi-block message; an over-long message refuses MessageTooLong and an over-long key refuses KeyTooLong before a byte is mixed. Constant-time tag comparison stays the Season's named horizon, supplied by the composer that verifies a MAC. Purely local — TEST keys and public vectors, no real identity key, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/hmac_sha256"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/hmac_sha256.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-hmac-sha256: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/hmac_sha256.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-hmac-sha256: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-hmac-sha256: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-hmac-sha256' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-hmac-sha256: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-hmac-sha256 line"
